WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.889
Chris Gore, Founder/Owner of FilmThreat/Author/Filmmaker:&nbsp;It’s
a true work of art and we don’t see a lot

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:08.990
of films that live up to the words&nbsp;true work
of art&nbsp;these days.&nbsp;

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:12.940
Film Courage: What are your 10 favorite movies?&nbsp;

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:23.110
Chris:&nbsp;My 10 favorite movies is an impossible
question. I would probably short circuit.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:28.780
I will tell you what one of my all-time favorite
independent films is the original&nbsp;Slacker&nbsp;directed

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:39.070
by&nbsp;Richard Linklater. That movie completely
breaks your standard mold of traditional storytelling

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:44.810
with three-act structure. It’s a day in
the life of Austin Texas where we meet all

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:51.060
types of people in all walks of life and they
kind of have intersecting lives. Each story

00:00:51.060 --> 00:01:00.559
kind of crosses over into the next and it’s
beautiful and absolutely sweet and inspiring.

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:08.860
That would be in my top 10 list. Another film
would be 2001: A Space Odyssey directed by

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:17.450
Stanley Kubrick is probably for me a perfect
film in the sense thatI saw it when I was

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:27.000
five years old as a kid and it blew my mind
and I probably watch it every few weeks and

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.650
by that I mean I keep it on in the background
while I'm working on other things but 2001:

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:43.670
A Space Odyssey is so inspiring is so beautiful
and then I mean it's a true work of art and

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:50.970
we don't see a lot of films that live up to
the words true work of art these days and

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:57.509
I tend to notice something new about it every
time I see it there's something fascinating

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:05.560
about that film and just the artistry the
the cinematography the the way the way that

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:11.190
these special effects in that film stand up
to today there was never a special edition

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:20.500
of 2001: A Space Odyssey although pro tip
if you if you get the film on iTunes they

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:25.590
will allow you to watch the film with the
original 1968 theatrical sound mix which I

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:34.520
strongly recommend but that film inspires
me it just I just kind of turn inward whenever

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:40.700
I watch that film and think about life and
death and the cosmos and the unknown and all

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:50.360
the unanswerable questions that we that religion
attempts to to answer for us that are truly

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:58.580
unanswerable only perhaps by faith and that
that film asks those questions without providing

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:07.870
an answer which I think is terrifying so I
love that movie the original star wars also

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:16.550
has to be on that list along with the empire
strikes back because

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:24.190
the time that they were made when you go back
to filmmaking in the 1970s those movies were

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:30.379
dark another favorite I would add to the list
see I'm actually going to get to the list

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:35.430
is taxi driver by Martin Scorcese and when
you look at the films that were being made

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:49.230
at the time in the 1970s they were very dark
the themes were this is a post-Nixon era when

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:57.049
in the shadow of all the cultural cultural
upheaval of the 1960s and the 70s were the

00:03:57.049 --> 00:04:06.299
hangover from that and still films were very
dark even in the realm of science fiction

00:04:06.299 --> 00:04:12.069
and George Lucas set out to to kind of flip
that and say I'm going make a fairy tale for

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:17.769
kids because there are no heroes in movies
today I mean at least that's what he thought

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:26.479
in the early 70s when he first began his journey
of writing the original star wars film so

00:04:26.479 --> 00:04:31.539
that one just stands out and when I say the
original star wars the original cut that was

00:04:31.539 --> 00:04:37.840
theatrical theatrically released on may 25th
1977 which I saw in a theater just a few days

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:43.099
later that original cut is just remarkable
and famously George Lucas did continue to

00:04:43.099 --> 00:04:49.599
cut even after it was only released on about
what a dozen screens initially and The Empire

00:04:49.599 --> 00:04:54.809
Strikes Back just sort of flipped that.

00:04:54.809 --> 00:05:00.069
Interestingly when George Lucas set about
working on the story forThe Empire Strikes

00:05:00.069 --> 00:05:07.210
Back and determined that his heavy which was
considered to be in the initial writing of

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:14.569
star wars a small role Darth Vader he considered
I'm going to make this the main hero's father

00:05:14.569 --> 00:05:23.889
and present a dilemma that is in order to
defeat the evil empire I have to harm my own

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:33.149
father and he actually consulted a child psychologist
to learn how this might affect children how

00:05:33.149 --> 00:05:38.729
the idea of this might affect children we
live in a time now where pop culture is consumed

00:05:38.729 --> 00:05:45.650
so quickly that a new Disney + series episode
will come out on a Wednesday and we've dissected

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:51.770
every part of it found every easter egg and
discussed it to death within 24 hours of its

00:05:51.770 --> 00:06:00.399
airing and the same goes with the film it
opens on a Thursday night, Friday and by Monday

00:06:00.399 --> 00:06:07.169
it's it's all been it's all been discussed
at the time that that films opened when I

00:06:07.169 --> 00:06:15.559
was a kid and seeing movies it was a discussion
that lasted for for some of these films years

00:06:15.559 --> 00:06:20.429
Logan's Run I would add to that list only
as a I don't really have a guilty pleasure

00:06:20.429 --> 00:06:29.490
I'm just going say Logan's Run because I like
the ideas espoused in that film yes it's shot

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:36.469
in a shopping mall in Dallas with futuristic
escalators and cheesy special effects that

00:06:36.469 --> 00:06:45.199
do not hold up but the performance by Michael
York and Jenny Agutter is just transcends

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:53.869
the poor special effects also an amazing score
but that film had a profound effect on me

00:06:53.869 --> 00:07:02.029
as a kid and I just love the ideas that was
there's there's a little bit of brave new

00:07:02.029 --> 00:07:10.459
world in that in Logan's Run kind of borrows
from that and the theatrical version of that

00:07:10.459 --> 00:07:16.800
movie with Michael York is very different
than the novel by William F. Nolan which I

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:23.119
read as a third grader and it really scarred
me because it's such a different the book

00:07:23.119 --> 00:07:31.449
and the movie are so vastly different I a
top 10 list for me is just like so difficult

00:07:31.449 --> 00:07:39.089
to put together I can talk about favorite
films and those kind of change with change

00:07:39.089 --> 00:07:49.629
with what my current thinking is about things
so this is an impossible question which is

00:07:49.629 --> 00:07:56.499
what I'm happy you're asking it's really impossible
for me for me to to respond to that with a

00:07:56.499 --> 00:08:02.869
top but but these are some of my favorites
and the and the reasons they and they they

00:08:02.869 --> 00:08:10.580
resonate for me to today now if Boyhood had
come out when you were a child do you think

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:15.770
that would replace Slacker is there something
nostalgic about some of the like because for

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:21.400
me I love Boyhood it's not that I didn't like
Slacker but I thought Boyhood was a masterpiece

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:27.529
so do you think you would have that would
have been your your one of your top ten yeah

00:08:27.529 --> 00:08:33.339
that's interesting point I do think that many
of the films from that are in my top list

00:08:33.339 --> 00:08:39.680
or movies that I saw at a younger age they
because they left such a profound impression

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:46.269
films like apocalypse now I think and I would
also say I would add to that list Pink Flamingos

00:08:46.269 --> 00:08:53.420
John Waters I saw that film when I was 16
years old. I got a car not because I cared

00:08:53.420 --> 00:09:00.649
about girls I got a car because I wanted to
go to movies so when I went to this rep this

00:09:00.649 --> 00:09:06.019
repertory theater did a John Waters tribute
and I saw Pink Flamingos and it was shocking

00:09:06.019 --> 00:09:10.889
to me I couldn't believe that people that
that structurally speaking I mean yeah I guess

00:09:10.889 --> 00:09:17.680
it follows 3x structure structure but there's
so many shocking elements in that film in

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:28.610
addition to Glen Milstead the drag queen Divine
who is probably one of the greatest drag queens

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:34.300
in cinematic history if that could be said
but that movie had a huge effect on me when

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:38.220
I was 16 and then I just like sort of went
down a rabbit hole I was fortunate enough

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:44.310
to work at a video store that had some of
his other films. Female Trouble might be a

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:52.690
personal favorite because it involves Christmas
day and cha cha heels which I thought was

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:56.870
so weird I mean John Waters kind of opened
my world up to like movies don't have to be

00:09:56.870 --> 00:10:02.209
this way they don't have to be Hollywood films
so I would say everything from like Slacker

00:10:02.209 --> 00:10:10.000
being this small indie movie made by this
kid Richard Linklater in Austin Texas that

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.519
just huge effect on me to like John Waters
who's been a true independent his entire career

00:10:15.519 --> 00:10:23.600
too truthfully George Lucas is an independent
filmmaker I mean yes he found funding in Hollywood

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:31.670
but his company Lucasfilm was not located
in Los Angeles he relocated to Marin County

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:38.639
in Northern California specifically to get
away from Hollywood I don't blame him but

00:10:38.639 --> 00:10:44.519
I would say that if there's a thread that
all the films that had an effect on me is

00:10:44.519 --> 00:10:53.940
they're all by very independent minded voices
and my fear with modern Hollywood as it becomes

00:10:53.940 --> 00:11:04.689
more corporatized is that those voices will
be lost and the ability to express oneself

00:11:04.689 --> 00:11:13.370
in through cinema because it's so expensive
that those voices will be lost and that's

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:22.089
what I have a real concern with and and I
see where the 70s filmmaking in Hollywood

00:11:22.089 --> 00:11:28.149
was very independently driven with this young
group of filmmakers everyone from Spielberg

00:11:28.149 --> 00:11:36.360
to Lucas and Scorsese and Coppola among others
young at the time. They were these new mavericks

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:43.089
in Hollywood and truly had independent voices
I don't know if we'll see an era like that

00:11:43.089 --> 00:11:50.980
again and it's it's disheartening to me and
I hope that there is some push to support

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:58.069
these independent filmmakers I do whatever
I can with my own outlet Film Threat we champion

00:11:58.069 --> 00:12:05.110
these small filmmakers but they just don't
get the support from the mass media like whatever

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:16.199
the latest Disney Marvel Pixar Star Wars thing
is which tends to consume all of the conversation

00:12:16.199 --> 00:12:24.769
of film for that week and so that's kind of
been my kind of my life's work through Film

00:12:24.769 --> 00:12:30.879
Threat is just to elevate these smaller movies
I just wonder if we'll see that same era again.

00:12:30.879 --> 00:12:31.879
